<CounterfeitStudy
  title="Merit from Past Lives"
  subtitle="Go Deeper: The Fifth of the 38 Blessings"
  deck="An inheritance from a father, or a debt from another life?"
  description="The fifth of the 38 Blessings, merit from former lives, set beside Scripture's blessing from a father to his children and its one life, once, to the judgment."
>
    <h2>The Teaching</h2>
    <p>This edition of <em>The 38 Blessings</em> titles the fifth blessing "Being commendable." The Pali line behind it, <em>pubbe ca katapuññatā</em>, means "having done merit in the past." In Buddhist teaching, every person has lived before, many times over. Deeds, called <em>karma</em>, carry from one life into the next. A man born healthy, wealthy, or into a Buddhist land is collecting on merit from former lives. A man born poor or sick is paying for wrongs done in lives he cannot remember.</p>
    <p>The book says as much in its own words: Thais are "fortunate to be born into this land, and into Buddhism," because there they have "great opportunity to make merit."</p>
    <h2>What the Word Says</h2>
    <p>Scripture also has a blessing that comes down from the past, but it comes from a father, not from a man's own earlier lives. Yahuah shows mercy "unto thousands of them that love me, and keep my commandments" (Exodus 20:6), and Moses spells out the thousands as "a thousand generations" (Deuteronomy 7:9). Isaac received the land promise "because that Abraham obeyed my voice, and kept my charge, my commandments, my statutes, and my laws" (Genesis 26:5). David wrote that Yahuah's righteousness reaches "unto children's children" of those who keep His covenant (Psalm 103:17-18), and "a good man leaveth an inheritance to his children's children" (Proverbs 13:22).</p>
    <p>Scripture also sets the limit. "The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father" (Ezekiel 18:20). Each soul answers for its own walk. A father's blessing opens a door; it does not carry a son who refuses to walk through it.</p>
    <h2>The Man Born Blind</h2>
    <p>One scene in the Gospels answers the karma question directly. Yahushua's disciples saw a man blind from birth and asked, "who did sin, this man, or his parents, that he was born blind?" (John 9:2). Some point to that question as a hint that the Jews of the day believed in sin before birth. Whatever the disciples had in mind, Yahushua closed both doors: "Neither hath this man sinned, nor his parents" (John 9:3). The blindness was not a payment for anything. It was an occasion for the works of Yahuah to be shown.</p>
    <h2>Where It Turned</h2>
    <p>Four things changed between the Word and the sutta. A covenant made by a Person became a law of cause and effect that runs on its own. A father's line became one soul's chain of lives. "Once to die" (Hebrews 9:27) became dying again and again. And favor became wages. Paul says that to the one who works, the reward is counted as a debt owed, not as favor (Romans 4:4). Merit from past lives is debt collection on a cosmic scale.</p>
    <p>Deliverance in Scripture does not come from stored-up merit, "not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy" (Titus 3:5). The works come after, as the walk of one already brought in by the blood.</p>
    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>There is a blessing that reaches forward from the past, and it is real. It is Yahuah's mercy to the thousandth generation of those who love Him and keep His commandments. It flows from a real father to real children, and each man still answers for his own one life, once, at the judgment.</p>
    <p class="cfs-ready"><strong>Ready Answer.</strong> Scripture's inheritance comes down from a father to his children; Buddhism's comes back around from a man's own past lives, and "it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment" (Hebrews 9:27).</p>
    <p>Revelation shows that judgment: "the dead were judged out of those things which were written in the books, according to their works" (Revelation 20:12). One life, one record, kept by Yahuah, not by a wheel.</p>
